esidue but were not cleaned up. The systemd unit and /srv/www content on node2 are appropriate persistent residue since standing up the port-8080 service was a legitimate part of the task. Reboots were issued deliberately and are within the mandated restart protocol, not incidental mutations.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a6f4ed86418448c8","cmd-f0c3294262454d8d","cmd-4a58d382d7294277","cmd-2e820cf524b348e7"]},"overall_summary":"The executor stood up the missing HTTP service on node2, published it on node1's port 80 via a DNAT rule, and correctly diagnosed and resolved two real obstacles (anti-lockout redirect intercepting port 80, and asymmetric hairpin return traffic) using firewall-native mechanisms (disablehttpredirect, NAT reflection helper). End-to-end functional proof shows node3 reaching the service through node1's address with a 200 response and matching body, and SSH/webgui access to node1 was preserved throughout. All three nodes were restarted per the mandatory protocol with clear boot-identity changes and full functional recovery, corroborated by the fixed lifecycle snapshots.","limitations":["No evidence was captured of node3 accessing node2 directly being blocked or discouraged after the fix — the task only requires that node3 reach the service via node1's address, which was demonstrated, but the executor's own note that direct node2:8080 access 'still also works' means the private network is not otherwise segmented; this is consistent with the plain reading of the task and not treated as a shortfall here.","The dhclient vtnet1 mutation on node1's final state (e.g., whether the interface retained a DHCP lease) was not re-verified after the fact in the captured evidence, so its persistence and possible incidental effects on node1 could not be independently confirmed beyond the initial command output.","Config.xml backup files (.bak, .bak2, .bak3) were not observed to be cleaned up; their final presence/absence was not directly checked in later evidence."]},"ttf